.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc \- declare and create an ordered map ordered by a custom comparison procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map whose keys custom_comparison_proc orders
instead of the default order for key_t.

The macro is cbmap_construct(3) plus a comparison procedure, and it uses
the default allocator. cbmap_construct_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead,
and cbmap_construct_full(3) takes both. cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) is the
same macro with a cleanup attribute, which destroys the map at the exit of
the scope.

A struct key_t needs this variant. Without a procedure, the map orders a key
type that it does not recognize by a raw comparison of the representation,
which reads padding bytes and orders a pointer member by its address.

cbmap_declare(3) followed by cbmap_init_cc(3) is the two-step form. Use it
when the caller must handle a creation failure and the failure must not be
fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key, and tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never gives it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key, a positive value when the
first key orders after the second key, and zero when the map must treat
the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update instead of an
insert, so the procedure defines both key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order must
not change while the map holds elements: the same pair of keys must always
compare in the same way, a comparison in the opposite direction must give
the opposite sign, and the order must be transitive. An inconsistent
procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree, after which the map
cannot find a key that it holds.

Because the procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, it can only
read a width that key_t fixes. The map therefore validates the width of an
incoming key against key_t and aligns its stored keys for key_t; the
procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro uses the default allocator
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3). The companion type variables follow
the scope of the handle, so a function that receives the handle needs
cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_construct_cc(releases, version, char *, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
